Thousands of the world's best apps trust RevenueCat to power in app purchases, manage customers, and grow revenue across iOS, Android and the web.

5
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:38,240
You can learn more at revenuecat dot com dot Let's get into the show.

6
00:00:40,480 --> 00:00:49,840
Hello, I'm your host, David Bernard, and my guest today is Anya Wysselska, founder of Rooted, an app that helps people overcome panic attacks.

7
00:00:50,640 --> 00:00:58,080
Anya bootstrapped Rooted from idea to over two million downloads without learning to code or hiring a single employee.

8
00:00:58,640 --> 00:01:11,120
On the podcast, I talk with Anya about the one small tweak that increased revenue five X, growing an app organically and how hiring an ASO consultant actually tanked downloads.

9
00:01:11,680 --> 00:01:14,240
Hey Anya, thank you so much for joining me on the podcast today.

10
00:01:14,560 --> 00:01:15,280
Thanks for having me.

11
00:01:15,840 --> 00:01:20,160
I've really been looking forward to talking to you since we met in person last year at MAU.

12
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:26,480
It's really cool how you found something that scratched your own itch and really turned it into a business.

13
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:31,520
So why don't you tell us a little bit about the founding of Rigid and why you chose built the app.

14
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:32,080
Yeah.

15
00:01:32,240 --> 00:01:35,440
So I do like to say it's like I approached it with a non MBA.

16
00:01:35,920 --> 00:01:40,160
You know, I didn't do any user research really or surveys to understand the market.

17
00:01:40,640 --> 00:01:42,560
It really was a response to my own need.

18
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:48,800
I was in my final year of school when I experienced a panic attack and I had no idea what they were beforehand.

19
00:01:49,200 --> 00:01:57,760
I thought that panic attacks were people that were just really stressed out or what they experienced and I just couldn't believe how actually terrifying it was.

20
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:00,160
And so I thought this can't be a panic attack.

21
00:02:00,240 --> 00:02:02,160
This is much worse than a panic attack can be.

22
00:02:02,400 --> 00:02:04,160
It turns out it is a panic attack.

23
00:02:04,480 --> 00:02:05,280
They are terrifying.

24
00:02:06,080 --> 00:02:24,160
I did take to the app store to look to see if there was something there that could help, but the apps that existed at the time under those keywords were things like hypnosis apps or apps that were more medical in nature, nothing that was very warm and comforting and what I really needed to see at the time.

25
00:02:24,560 --> 00:02:26,080
And I was far away from home.

26
00:02:26,480 --> 00:02:27,760
You know, I was on student loans.

27
00:02:27,920 --> 00:02:29,120
I didn't have a family doctor.

28
00:02:29,600 --> 00:02:32,160
I didn't have the resources to do see a traditional therapist.

29
00:02:32,240 --> 00:02:39,120
So unfortunately, it took me a while to get over the panic attacks, to manage my anxiety better and get to a better place.

30
00:02:39,840 --> 00:02:51,840
And so during that process of healing, I was thinking, this is actually the tool that helps whenever I came across a new tool that helps, and I was thinking, how do I get this all into one tool that I can just keep on me every day?

31
00:02:52,400 --> 00:02:55,680
So I'm not taking these heavy textbooks around campus with me.

32
00:02:55,840 --> 00:02:58,160
I'm just, I have this one thing on me all the time.

33
00:02:58,720 --> 00:03:00,960
So that's how the idea for Rooded started.

34
00:03:01,680 --> 00:03:04,080
So how did you go from this app needs to exist?

35
00:03:04,560 --> 00:03:23,200
And it's funny because this podcast isn't necessarily targeted at the average person who likes has an app idea, but I think there are a lot of people in the industry, you know, whether they work in marketing and a bigger app or they're an independent developer and they've released five different apps and none of them have really stuck, but a lot of people think, oh, this app should exist.

36
00:03:24,080 --> 00:03:30,560
And how did you go from this app should exist to then actually launching an app, especially as a non-technical.

37
00:03:32,240 --> 00:03:37,440
So at first I just started drawing what I thought I needed in my notebooks.

38
00:03:37,680 --> 00:03:45,280
So it was pen on paper, very simple stuff, but really with a lot of emphasis on like what that user journey should be.

39
00:03:45,680 --> 00:03:48,800
And that's something I was more familiar with than coding or programming.

40
00:03:49,600 --> 00:03:52,720
And for background, I studied international relations in school.

41
00:03:53,200 --> 00:03:58,560
So the elements that probably did help me were things like communication and storytelling stuff like that.

42
00:03:58,960 --> 00:04:03,360
So I was very careful about the language in Rooted and really wanted it to be something simple.

43
00:04:03,680 --> 00:04:07,120
When I was looking at other apps, sometimes things can feel really cluttered.

44
00:04:07,200 --> 00:04:11,200
So you'll see even today with Rooted, it's very simple user interface, very simple design.

45
00:04:11,520 --> 00:04:13,280
So that's what I was drawing in my notebooks.

46
00:04:13,600 --> 00:04:23,680
And then I took that to an agency to get a quote and they quoted me something like 40 K and there was just no way I could afford that.

47
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:27,600
And I was like, holy, I'm going to have to learn so much more if I want to do this.

48
00:04:28,400 --> 00:04:47,680
So it took a lot of persistence in the face of not knowing anything about this subject and really researching, watching YouTube videos, learning what wireframes were, learning about different programming languages, not that I was even wanting to learn it myself, but I just needed to even understand what went into making an app.

49
00:04:48,080 --> 00:04:49,120
So that's how it started.

50
00:04:49,520 --> 00:04:56,400
Yeah, it's funny because a lot of people don't know and you and I were just talking before the podcast that I am a non technical founder as well.

51
00:04:57,200 --> 00:05:22,560
And that's how I started too, as I actually started learning to program and realized oh geez like this is going to take a year or two to get good and i need an ass out soon i'd like borrowed money from family to found my company so yeah i ended up finding a contractor so after you saw that $40,000 bid from an agency just wasn't going to work what did you do next So it was quite a setback.

52
00:05:23,200 --> 00:05:28,320
I was at the time doing marketing and comms for different startups in town.

53
00:05:28,800 --> 00:05:37,120
So I think I was just very vocal about what I wanted to do and I would share with people what I'm really focused on outside of the work I was doing with them.

54
00:05:37,680 --> 00:05:46,320
And over time, this student developer came to me and he was like, hey, like, are you still planning to do that app you mentioned for panic attacks?

55
00:05:46,480 --> 00:05:47,760
And I was like, yeah, totally.

56
00:05:47,920 --> 00:05:48,800
I was stoked.

57
00:05:49,280 --> 00:05:56,960
And he was like, oh, I've done all these co-ops and I haven't really done any work for a company that's actually doing good for society or the world.

58
00:05:57,440 --> 00:06:02,480
And I feel like that would be more fun for one of my last hurrahs before I go to work look for full time work and stuff.

59
00:06:02,880 --> 00:06:03,680
That's how we started.

60
00:06:03,920 --> 00:06:15,520
I learned a lot about how to communicate better with developers too, because that's something, you know, that was quite foreign to me and just kind of realized where the communication could be improved.

61
00:06:15,760 --> 00:06:20,880
When I look back at that first process of how we did it, I was sending JPEG maps of stuff.

62
00:06:21,040 --> 00:06:22,560
You know, there was no Figma back then.

63
00:06:23,120 --> 00:06:27,360
It was very low-tech, but yeah, we did it first in React native.

64
00:06:27,760 --> 00:06:30,720
That was the first language that Rooted was initially programmed in.

65
00:06:31,920 --> 00:06:35,520
So you launched and started to kind of slowly get traction.

66
00:06:35,920 --> 00:06:37,280
How did the team evolve over time?

67
00:06:37,600 --> 00:06:40,800
Did you keep working with that student developer for a while or how did that progress?

68
00:06:41,440 --> 00:06:47,520
Yeah, we worked together until he found a full time job and I had to learn also how to adapt to that.

69
00:06:47,760 --> 00:06:51,040
There wasn't really full time work at Rooted in terms of monetizing.

70
00:06:51,440 --> 00:06:53,600
I had some paywalls, but it was really hidden.

71
00:06:53,760 --> 00:06:57,120
Like the point was to provide that panic attack relief immediately.

72
00:06:57,600 --> 00:06:58,560
So we don't have ads.

73
00:06:59,120 --> 00:07:02,800
All the paid content was very., again, not obvious.

74
00:07:03,280 --> 00:07:08,000
So there wasn't full time hours either, let alone revenue to pay for full time hours.

75
00:07:08,160 --> 00:07:11,120
So we did work together until, you know, he had a different opportunity.

76
00:07:11,440 --> 00:07:19,760
And then since then I've been working with various different contractors on the development side here and there, people jump on, contribute what they can.

77
00:07:20,640 --> 00:07:25,680
And now as Root had grown, there are people who want to stay around a bit more.

78
00:07:25,920 --> 00:07:30,400
But I think it's just like with employees, it's not guaranteed that someone can stay around forever.

79
00:07:31,040 --> 00:07:34,800
The intent is always that you join a company, you're excited by it and you're staying around.

80
00:07:35,440 --> 00:07:40,480
But over the years with Rooted, I mean, there's the war in Ukraine, some developers had to leave because of that.

81
00:07:40,960 --> 00:07:43,760
During COVID, developers had to leave to focus on health and stuff.

82
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:47,760
So just like with employees, it's been a mix of working with different people.

83
00:07:48,240 --> 00:07:49,520
I've been the one constant really.

84
00:07:50,160 --> 00:07:53,120
I can totally relate in fifteen years running my company.

85
00:07:53,280 --> 00:07:55,360
I never ended up hiring a single employee.

86
00:07:55,680 --> 00:08:02,720
So I ran the company and saw millions of downloads on the App Store and millions of dollars in gross revenue without ever hiring an employee.

87
00:08:02,880 --> 00:08:13,360
And I think a lot of people don't realize just how much a determined person can achieve by force of will and, you know, hiring as needed on a contracting basis.

88
00:08:13,600 --> 00:08:16,480
And I eventually did start partnering with developers.

89
00:08:16,640 --> 00:08:23,680
I had a few projects that kind of went sideways where I spent tens of thousands of dollars with contractors and wasn't getting the results.

90
00:08:24,080 --> 00:08:30,720
And have you had any experiences like that where you are working with a contractor and you just have to like cut them loose and just didn't work?

91
00:08:31,280 --> 00:08:31,360
out.

92
00:08:31,920 --> 00:08:32,240
Totally.

93
00:08:32,400 --> 00:08:32,480
Yeah.

94
00:08:32,880 --> 00:08:40,800
So typically I work with individuals versus agencies, but for the Watch app we worked with an agency.

95
00:08:41,520 --> 00:08:49,360
It wasn't so terrible, but I just realized later that with another developer looking at it that it was kind of like the lipstick on a pig type coding situation.

96
00:08:49,600 --> 00:08:51,600
There's no way to know that as a technical founder.

97
00:08:51,680 --> 00:08:57,440
And I found that I was like, built a lot more than the quote in the end and it just wasn't too pleasant of an experience.

98
00:08:57,680 --> 00:09:00,480
So with them, that was a bit easier because it was just once that.

99
00:09:01,200 --> 00:09:03,120
first version was done, we just stopped working together.

100
00:09:03,600 --> 00:09:12,480
But I also have had a few nightmare contractor stories where they just are telling a different story during the interviews and as you get to know each other.

101
00:09:12,720 --> 00:09:21,600
And then once you actually jump into work, oh man, I mean, one person accidentally sent twenty thousand messages to a small group of people, like push notifications.

102
00:09:21,920 --> 00:09:22,400
Oh my god.

103
00:09:22,880 --> 00:09:25,760
They didn't even like act apologetic or anything afterwards.

104
00:09:26,160 --> 00:09:27,360
It's like this could not happen.

105
00:09:27,920 --> 00:09:28,960
And they were just like, ugh.

106
00:09:29,200 --> 00:09:38,160
You know, and I was just like, wow, like different attitude because I was so obsessed with the user experience that it's important to work with others who are also obsessed with user experience, you know?

107
00:09:38,480 --> 00:09:39,360
So yeah, that's one.

108
00:09:40,080 --> 00:09:41,600
It was just kind of the school of hard knocks.

109
00:09:41,840 --> 00:09:43,200
I've had plenty of those experiences.

110
00:09:43,360 --> 00:09:53,040
And if you're a non technical founder trying to build an app, you know, I know there's a lot of people listening to this podcast who are in marketing and maybe think, ah, I could build an app, but I don't know how to code.

111
00:09:53,840 --> 00:09:56,480
It can work, but there's going to be struggles along the way.

112
00:09:57,120 --> 00:10:03,760
Now that you've had over two million downloads and are just finishing I've gotten a lot of tension for the app.

113
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:06,800
I imagine you've had some interest from investors.

114
00:10:07,200 --> 00:10:08,960
Have you raised any funding?

115
00:10:09,200 --> 00:10:10,880
Are you interested in raising funding?

116
00:10:11,040 --> 00:10:12,880
And if so, why or why not?

117
00:10:13,280 --> 00:10:22,080
I think it goes back to just being really obsessed with the problem in the space and I haven't found that I've done all the experiments that I want to do yet.

118
00:10:22,800 --> 00:10:29,680
And thankfully, once I started iterating with the paywalls and stuff, we started earning a bit more revenue.

119
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:35,840
And then I'm now able to work on these tests and these goals without necessarily getting outside capital.

120
00:10:36,480 --> 00:10:40,400
So I think it comes back to A, there's still so much I want to do.

121
00:10:40,560 --> 00:10:49,600
Like, I haven't hit a wall yet, which where I think that getting investors' perspectives and different strategies could be really helpful at that time, I'm just not there yet.

122
00:10:50,320 --> 00:10:56,160
And on the other hand, the problems that I am experiencing, I'm not sure that throwing money at them will solve them.

123
00:10:56,960 --> 00:11:10,480
So as an example, team communication, like let's say someone something's not quite working with the developers and the contractors and the communication, throwing in a more maybe expensive developer into the mix is not always going to solve the solution.

124
00:11:10,720 --> 00:11:14,080
Sometimes it's actually removing someone and fixing internal processes.

125
00:11:14,320 --> 00:11:16,240
So that's just kind of how I look at it.

126
00:11:16,800 --> 00:11:24,240
I also had an early conversation with an investor who buys apps and different small businesses that are distressed.

127
00:11:25,040 --> 00:11:33,520
And in hearing him chat about it, he would say, you know, there's founders that think that their problem is the biggest problem in the world, but really, it's not at all.

128
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:37,920
And so I buy these businesses and then I'll throw in a solution because it's easy for me to do that.

129
00:11:38,320 --> 00:11:41,520
And I was like, wow, I'm so determined not to be that person then.

130
00:11:42,160 --> 00:11:46,960
Now that I know how it's like, given your perspective, it could be my problem can actually be a very small problem.

131
00:11:47,600 --> 00:11:56,320
And so I really try to look at my problems when they feel big and try to look at them from different perspectives and sort of break it down and think, okay, what do I really need here?

132
00:11:56,640 --> 00:12:00,480
And nine times out of ten, the answer just hasn't been straight up capital.

133
00:12:01,280 --> 00:12:03,440
It's been other issues that I needed to resolve.

134
00:12:04,080 --> 00:12:05,200
That's such a great perspective.

135
00:12:05,360 --> 00:12:16,480
I think too many people look at money as a solution and thinking throwing money at something will fix it or accelerate it, move things faster, you grow faster, you know, all those things.

136
00:12:16,960 --> 00:12:22,800
But it's a really great way to think about it that when you break things down, there's just so many problems that you can solve.

137
00:12:23,040 --> 00:12:29,360
And it's really cool to see how resourceful you've been in just solving them through brute force and through force of will.

138
00:12:29,840 --> 00:12:36,960
Again, it's pretty incredible that you've gotten to two million downloads without outside capital, you know, starting with a student developer.

139
00:12:37,120 --> 00:12:42,400
I know App Store Search and ASO has played a big role in getting those downloads.

140
00:12:42,800 --> 00:12:53,200
Can you tell me a little bit about how you thought about ASO early on and how you got those kind of early downloads that then started to build that snowball that grew you to where you are today?

141
00:12:53,680 --> 00:12:53,760
Yeah.

142
00:12:54,000 --> 00:13:00,560
So when I first started rooted and you had the core button, that red button in the app, the rooter for panic attacks, I knew that that would probably be.

143
00:13:01,040 --> 00:13:04,000
one of the most impactful features for users because it was for me too.

144
00:13:04,800 --> 00:13:12,800
And if my app was going to resonate with folks that I again hypothesized without user research, but, you know, just based on really being familiar with the problem.

145
00:13:13,840 --> 00:13:20,000
So I looked up the keyword panic attack, saw that it was not very competitive, pretty low volume.

146
00:13:20,240 --> 00:13:24,400
And then I looked up anxiety on the other hand, and that was more competition, more volume.

147
00:13:25,360 --> 00:13:29,920
And I was so tempted to go for anxiety because I'm like, this is also anxiety.

148
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:35,680
And as a founder, you know, sometimes you have founder it is you think that other people will see what you see in your own company right away.

149
00:13:36,080 --> 00:13:37,200
That's not always the case though.

150
00:13:37,520 --> 00:13:47,120
So I did stick with panic attacks and even though the volume was much lower back in the day, when people did find rooted with that keyword, they really got what they were looking for.

151
00:13:47,760 --> 00:13:53,200
And so the user reviews that were being left from the very beginning were so incredibly impactful.

152
00:13:53,760 --> 00:13:55,920
People were sharing how rooted's changing their life.

153
00:13:56,160 --> 00:14:03,040
And I remember seeing that for the first time being like, wow, like this really did speak to others in the way that it did to me.

154
00:14:03,280 --> 00:14:04,480
And that was a really cool experience.

155
00:14:04,560 --> 00:14:06,080
And that, of course, helps with ASO.

156
00:14:06,400 --> 00:14:19,680
As you know, if users have the same experience when they're from search to being on your product page to downloading and then leaving a review, especially if they're leaving a review with the keywords on your product page, that all really ties in to ASO.

157
00:14:19,840 --> 00:14:22,240
And I think what Apple and Google see in the back end too.

158
00:14:22,800 --> 00:14:25,200
Did you use a tool for that ASO research?

159
00:14:25,760 --> 00:14:30,640
Yeah, at the time, I think it was either Sensor Tower or App Figures.

160
00:14:30,640 --> 00:15:04,560
app figures yeah there's so many great tools to do that keyword discovery and it's really insightful again i think a lot of people miss that being a really great fit for a keyword that has less volume can be a lot better than being you know result 10 for the much higher traffic keywords yeah it's so great that you decided to focus on that more niche but just such a better fit for the app early on i know you did some experimentation how did that go experimenting with it so it went well in the sense that it helped me figure out which keywords to really focus on.

161
00:15:05,280 --> 00:15:12,000
And then at one point I did work with an outside contractor for that and it was a really bad experience.

162
00:15:12,480 --> 00:15:20,080
I went from like ranking number two for panic attacks, number three or four for anxiety, to then just like tanking.

163
00:15:20,720 --> 00:15:27,280
And I remember looking at the graphs and I'm like, oh my God, this is the most I've invested into ASO, you know, not doing it myself.

164
00:15:27,680 --> 00:15:28,960
And it's just going horribly.

165
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:34,640
Hopefully, once the contract ended, we were able to build up again by really reverting to the original.

166
00:15:35,200 --> 00:15:44,640
So now Rooderd ranges number one for panic attacks, number two or three for anxiety, depending on the day, and it just brings in a lot of volume of downloads that way.

167
00:15:45,520 --> 00:16:04,800
And yeah, I'm not saying that all outside contracting is bad, of course, like I use contractors and it works really well sometimes, but that was a funny experience because when people don't believe how bad it is, I show them the graph and then they're like, wow, I think I even used it at the conferencece when we met in one of my slides because it was quite an impactful image.

168
00:16:05,440 --> 00:16:09,360
Do you remember what they changed that made such a dramatic difference?

169
00:16:10,160 --> 00:16:14,400
They had started off with Bitcoin keywords and stuff that just wasn't even related.

170
00:16:14,640 --> 00:16:20,240
And I think they had some process in mind, but I was just losing downloads by day, losing revenue by day.

171
00:16:20,400 --> 00:16:21,600
I was like, I can't do it.

172
00:16:22,080 --> 00:16:27,600
They were probably chasing higher traffic keywords that weren't as good of a fit for your app.

173
00:16:28,560 --> 00:16:28,960
Holly.

174
00:16:29,360 --> 00:16:30,800
It's crazy that someone would charge more.

175
00:16:31,120 --> 00:16:34,160
money for the Tension Bad strategy.

176
00:16:34,560 --> 00:16:34,560
Yeah.

177
00:16:35,120 --> 00:16:35,200
Wow.

178
00:16:35,760 --> 00:16:35,840
Yeah.

179
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:39,760
They came recommended though, or at least their website made it seem that way.

180
00:16:39,920 --> 00:16:41,360
So yeah, lots of thoughts on that.

181
00:16:41,920 --> 00:16:42,000
Yeah.

182
00:16:42,800 --> 00:16:47,120
Related to App Store optimization, have you run any Apple search ads?

183
00:16:47,280 --> 00:16:52,720
I know anxiety related keywords are probably bit up pretty high by things like home and headspace and other things.

184
00:16:53,040 --> 00:16:54,880
Has that been something you've tried?

185
00:16:55,120 --> 00:16:56,560
And if so, has it been successful?

186
00:16:57,200 --> 00:16:57,280
Yeah.

187
00:16:57,520 --> 00:17:02,240
So some branded keywords as well as panic attack related keywords.

188
00:17:02,480 --> 00:17:03,200
And yeah, it has.

189
00:17:03,840 --> 00:17:08,160
I have yet to really turn up the volume though in terms of spend.

190
00:17:08,480 --> 00:17:10,080
And that's something I'm looking forward to doing.

191
00:17:10,240 --> 00:17:15,040
I've been focusing a lot on the in-app funnel right now.

192
00:17:15,440 --> 00:17:23,840
And I'm looking forward to experimenting more with ads, but I did have some folks look at our Apple search ads as well and give suggestions.

193
00:17:24,320 --> 00:17:27,840
And there's also those calls that you can do with Apple where they give suggestions.

194
00:17:28,080 --> 00:17:30,640
I'm not sure which is more effective, the third party.

195
00:17:31,200 --> 00:17:39,280
people probably, but we have done a bit of that, but really just maintaining almost like the bare minimum for now before we kind of turn up that volume of spend.

196
00:17:39,840 --> 00:17:42,160
You mentioned kind of dialing in the funnel.

197
00:17:42,400 --> 00:17:48,640
What are some ways in onboarding and other ways that you have been able to dial in that funnel?

198
00:17:49,280 --> 00:17:54,000
So one really easy strategy that now everyone's doing, but this was back in 2020.

199
00:17:54,800 --> 00:17:57,280
So it was probably becoming a trend when I saw it.

200
00:17:57,680 --> 00:17:58,720
I'm not saying I started it.

201
00:17:58,880 --> 00:18:00,160
People much smarter than me were doing this.

202
00:18:00,560 --> 00:18:04,400
It's basically putting the paywall near the beginning of the onboarding process, right?

203
00:18:04,560 --> 00:18:09,440
And there's a lot of debate as to which screen it should be in the onboarding process, but mine was just so hidden.

204
00:18:09,920 --> 00:18:21,280
I was so worried that people would be turned off by a paywall that they would just react poorly to it, given that Rooted focuses on panic attacks and anxiety, a paywall in your face might not be great.

205
00:18:21,840 --> 00:18:28,400
It turns out, though, people are so used to seeing it that we got almost no negative feedback on it, increased our revenue by five times.

206
00:18:28,960 --> 00:18:29,040
Wow.

207
00:18:29,680 --> 00:18:30,240
It was incredible.

208
00:18:30,800 --> 00:18:45,120
It went from being something that I could just keep working on by putting in the revenue monthly to then being something where I could really have more contractors, way more of a vision for Rooted 2 because of that one simple change.

209
00:18:45,520 --> 00:18:46,800
So that was really incredible.

210
00:18:46,960 --> 00:18:55,040
I would say that's like probably the best example because since then it's been smaller iterations that do affect things but by way smaller percentage.

211
00:18:55,600 --> 00:18:58,800
So things like setting up reminders within the onboarding as well.

212
00:18:59,040 --> 00:19:00,480
We find that that's one of the biggest.

213
00:19:01,120 --> 00:19:08,800
indicators of retention is if someone sets up a reminder, they're being notified every day to come back to breathe, journal or visualize with rooted.

214
00:19:09,360 --> 00:19:16,320
Another thing that we sort of want to experiment a bit more with is the Oh push notifications.

215
00:19:16,640 --> 00:19:21,840
That was another one where we now send out daily quotes to folks for the first seven days when they download rooted.

216
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:27,120
And then after that, it is a premium feature, but that's something that's been received very positively as well.

217
00:19:27,440 --> 00:19:38,080
So little things like that to really focus on increasing both the download to following through with using rooted as well as install to trial and then trial to conversion.

218
00:19:38,480 --> 00:19:40,240
Those are the core metrics that we focus on.

219
00:19:40,880 --> 00:19:42,240
That's such a great story.

220
00:19:42,960 --> 00:19:47,520
It's tough and I've done the same in my apps and they're actually not performing very well right now.

221
00:19:47,680 --> 00:19:53,200
I've been more focused on my job than my business these last couple of years, but the same thing.

222
00:19:53,360 --> 00:20:05,200
It's like so many folks, it's almost like you're scared to give people a bad experience and then especially I think when you're a little more mission driven or really care, it's such bad experience.

223
00:20:05,360 --> 00:20:08,720
And I don't want to interrupt the mission, the experience that people are getting.

224
00:20:08,880 --> 00:20:11,200
But at the end of the day, look at what happened.

225
00:20:11,360 --> 00:20:16,960
You 5x your revenue and you're able to do so much more for the users who are actually getting value.

226
00:20:17,200 --> 00:20:23,760
And so that's an incredible story of one simple change, but taking a big swing and then seeing how it turned out.

227
00:20:24,400 --> 00:20:28,000
I did want to get back to the early growth.

228
00:20:28,400 --> 00:21:05,120
I know something you've thought a lot about and again something I've thought a lot about as a non-technical co-founder I'm not head down and code all the time and so I'm just thinking how can I get attention how can I get attention it seems like you very much have that mindset like people need to be aware of your app so you've done a lot of PR and storytelling and gotten featured by Apple so tell me about how you think about press and just getting attention broadly for the app yeah so I think at the very beginning I knew that you know without an advertising budget, what I could do is write a great story, tie it back to a specific date, right?

229
00:21:05,440 --> 00:21:09,600
Because press really does love having a story that ties back to a world event.

230
00:21:09,920 --> 00:21:24,720
So I launched on World Mental Health Day and my strategy there since Rooded had no background yet, no traction or validation, I sent it to local folks and journalists and typically I think that works almost anywhere you are in the world.

231
00:21:25,120 --> 00:21:27,440
Folks really do want to promote their local business stories.

232
00:21:27,600 --> 00:21:29,680
So that helped in getting those first few hundred.

233
00:21:30,400 --> 00:21:37,040
And then later, you know, Rudd started making these different lists like on Healthline and an article came out in Glamour magazine and stuff.

234
00:21:37,280 --> 00:21:46,080
So then I would basically amp that up and send it to even more press in the States, even in Europe, to get more stories behind it.

235
00:21:46,640 --> 00:21:54,880
And some other examples are, you know, with the World Cup, we talked a lot about how athletes are opening up about experiencing more anxiety and panic attacks.

236
00:21:55,040 --> 00:21:57,040
And we launched a visualization for athletes.

237
00:21:57,440 --> 00:21:58,960
So that was a story that did quite well.

238
00:21:59,440 --> 00:22:00,240
Again going back to.

239
00:22:00,400 --> 00:22:02,080
to World Mental Health Day.

240
00:22:02,240 --> 00:22:07,840
Pretty much every October 10th, we'll do a big press release and an update on what's going on with Rooted.

241
00:22:08,320 --> 00:22:08,880
That's very cool.

242
00:22:09,120 --> 00:22:15,280
I think I saw somewhere you actually have a calendar where you've planned out for an entire year what you're going to talk about every month.

243
00:22:15,600 --> 00:22:16,160
Tell me about that.

244
00:22:16,640 --> 00:22:16,640
Yeah.

245
00:22:16,960 --> 00:22:25,680
So in December or early January, typically we plan out basically for every month of the year what new thing is happening in Rooted.

246
00:22:26,240 --> 00:22:35,440
So we do one big update per month and other than that, still like smaller updates week weekly throughout the year, but that big update, I do try to tell a story about it.

247
00:22:36,080 --> 00:22:41,600
So for example, May today is May first and it is Mental Health Month internationally.

248
00:22:42,480 --> 00:22:55,040
One user request that we got rather frequently is to have the rooter button, which is that panic attack button for the prompts to play on autoplay so that someone can close their eyes and listen to these reassuring prompts right now.

249
00:22:55,200 --> 00:22:57,280
You just click next and you listen to the next one.

250
00:22:57,680 --> 00:23:00,480
So it's a simple thing, but really, it does.

251
00:23:00,480 --> 00:23:13,200
It does bring back even more accessibility for the user who might have, you know, sometimes when you're in the middle of a panic attack, you could have blurred vision, you can have trouble even navigating something, even though we try to keep rooted very simple.

252
00:23:13,760 --> 00:23:15,760
It's really important to keep it as simple as possible.

253
00:23:16,480 --> 00:23:23,520
So with this feature, we're able to tell a story about how you can really close your eyes and let the rooter do its work while you just sit back and relax.

254
00:23:23,840 --> 00:23:25,760
And we're going to do an in-app event about it.

255
00:23:25,920 --> 00:23:27,040
We're going to do prep about it.

256
00:23:27,280 --> 00:23:29,040
So that's an example for me.

257
00:23:29,520 --> 00:23:41,040
And then we do that all through basically September, because September is when the new iOS updates come out, then we do want to try to incorporate something from those iOS updates into Rooted for October and November.

258
00:23:41,440 --> 00:23:45,040
December is typically holiday focused and January is New Year focused.

259
00:23:45,200 --> 00:23:52,240
So it's really between February to let's say September that we have these monthly stories that we already know what they'll be every month.

260
00:23:52,640 --> 00:23:53,280
That's so cool.

261
00:23:53,440 --> 00:24:00,560
I think a lot of apps sleep on this that there are stories that you can tell with your app with new features with Apple.

262
00:24:00,560 --> 00:24:32,640
you know with apple releasing new features and it's one of those untapped things where if all you're focused on is paid marketing or just you know it is hard to get placement these days with press because they're just inundated everybody but when you tell a good story people want to help spread that story and it's really cool to see rooted having gotten covered so much is there a particular press placement or some form of attention that you got organically that's spiked downloads like crazy and been the best you've ever gotten.

263
00:24:33,360 --> 00:24:33,760
Not really.

264
00:24:34,240 --> 00:24:36,240
It's really by trial and error.

265
00:24:36,560 --> 00:24:38,000
Everything moves the dial a little bit.

266
00:24:38,240 --> 00:24:39,280
Nothing like so extreme.

267
00:24:39,680 --> 00:24:46,240
Some day, like, you know, on World Mental Health Day, for example, some of those days we'll get a big bump, but then it does calm down afterwards.

268
00:24:46,480 --> 00:24:54,640
So it's really about just keep going, keep doing it over and over again until those bumps become the norm because you're doing it well and you're doing it often.

269
00:24:55,280 --> 00:24:55,360
Yeah.

270
00:24:55,680 --> 00:24:56,160
That's really cool.

271
00:24:56,320 --> 00:24:57,760
And then what about getting pitched by Apple?

272
00:24:58,080 --> 00:25:00,560
I know recently you were selected as an editor's choice.

273
00:25:01,040 --> 00:25:02,960
Have the Apple features been really helpful?

274
00:25:03,520 --> 00:25:03,840
Totally.

275
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:04,080
Yes.

276
00:25:04,480 --> 00:25:08,960
So, when I started out, I had no idea how to get them right.

277
00:25:09,200 --> 00:25:20,720
I was just submitting submission after submission after submission and I would try to connect with other app founders who have been featured and I just didn't have much luck and unfortunately, I don't have any magic sauce here.

278
00:25:20,880 --> 00:25:30,800
I just kept doing the submission, submission, submission until finally one day I heard back and this was probably like fifteen submissions later saying, hey, prepare some artwork.

279
00:25:31,280 --> 00:25:37,040
because you might be featured and that was like a huge deal and I was so excited and that's kind of how it started.

280
00:25:37,680 --> 00:25:41,680
And I'd say they do have a big impact because I'm not doing advertising at this time.

281
00:25:42,320 --> 00:25:44,240
So yes, they do have a big impact.

282
00:25:44,800 --> 00:25:49,200
We see a lot of increase in browse installs during those days.

283
00:25:49,840 --> 00:25:51,520
What's your typical kind of balance?

284
00:25:51,760 --> 00:26:01,920
Because I know ASO plays a big role, but now that you've been featured so much, is it a pretty like even split between getting your downloads coming from being featured and ASO?

285
00:26:02,240 --> 00:26:03,120
It depends on the month.

286
00:26:03,280 --> 00:26:07,360
It depends what type of feature was going on that month and in what market.

287
00:26:08,080 --> 00:26:16,080
So the US is our largest market and by far, if we're featured in the US, that's quite a significant bump as a Canadian app though.

288
00:26:16,160 --> 00:26:18,000
We're often featured in Canada, which is also great.

289
00:26:18,400 --> 00:26:20,400
I think it's a bigger market than most people assume.

290
00:26:21,040 --> 00:26:22,400
So it's also awesome here.

291
00:26:23,200 --> 00:26:27,360
And then in Europe, I find it's like maybe less of an impact there.

292
00:26:28,080 --> 00:26:30,320
But yeah, I mean, there have been some days where.

293
00:26:31,440 --> 00:26:40,640
Rooted has, you know, had so many installs within a few days that for the average for the whole month is like that there's more installs by browse than search.

294
00:26:41,040 --> 00:26:56,000
But on the flip side, there are also days where organically Rooted gets a lot of traction, be it from an article or, you know, a world event, like when the war in Ukraine started, we actually were number seven in all of Ukraine and Russia for like weeks at a time.

295
00:26:56,560 --> 00:26:57,840
And that was completely organic.

296
00:26:58,000 --> 00:26:59,840
No marketing, no features, as far as I know.

297
00:27:00,080 --> 00:27:03,120
I'm aware, like we tried to find out and there's no sign of that.

298
00:27:03,360 --> 00:27:06,000
So these bumps happen both ways, I guess.

299
00:27:06,480 --> 00:27:14,000
We talked a lot about the App Store, but from both a download and then revenue standpoint, what's kind of the split bin between iOS and Android?

300
00:27:14,640 --> 00:27:16,480
Pretty consistent with what others see, I think.

301
00:27:16,800 --> 00:27:27,440
While there's more downloads on Android, there is more revenue coming from iOS, probably like a 75 to 25 percent split or 70 to 30.

302
00:27:28,080 --> 00:27:28,160
Wow.

303
00:27:28,880 --> 00:27:28,960
Yeah.

304
00:27:29,520 --> 00:27:30,720
That's a pretty typical Yeah.

305
00:27:30,720 --> 00:27:30,800
Pretty difficult.

306
00:27:30,960 --> 00:27:31,040
Yeah.

307
00:27:31,440 --> 00:27:39,200
Have you experimented with like lower prices country by country or anything like that to kind of take advantage of some of those Android downloads?

308
00:27:39,680 --> 00:27:40,880
I have, believe it or not.

309
00:27:41,040 --> 00:27:45,520
I actually got feedback from someone in Brazil saying that my price is too low.

310
00:27:46,480 --> 00:27:47,200
So, wow.

311
00:27:48,080 --> 00:27:50,720
Definitely experimented and tried all sorts of things.

312
00:27:51,440 --> 00:27:55,680
There just seems to be less of an adoption of subscriptions in general.

313
00:27:56,160 --> 00:27:58,400
But I think that there is way more that I can do.

314
00:27:58,960 --> 00:28:00,640
And I think stepping back and sort of.

315
00:28:01,200 --> 00:28:06,640
re-looking at pricing every now and then is something that everyone should do and I should do more of it too.

316
00:28:07,120 --> 00:28:19,920
And as you've got your funnel delved in and you know, you've kind of got this organic loop going, have you started to experiment or have you experimented in the past with paid marketing or have you just relied solely on the organic traction?

317
00:28:20,320 --> 00:28:21,680
So I did experiment in the past.

318
00:28:22,000 --> 00:28:28,960
I sort of took a risk and working with an agency did quite a bit of advertising for a bit.

319
00:28:30,400 --> 00:28:32,000
a local agency here in BC.

320
00:28:32,240 --> 00:28:35,680
And so they were awesome, but I just think the app wasn't ready.

321
00:28:36,240 --> 00:28:37,280
That funnel wasn't there.

322
00:28:37,440 --> 00:28:41,440
It was before I had that additional paywall that I mentioned that really helped with conversions.

323
00:28:41,920 --> 00:28:46,960
And actually now we're pretty close to being ready to experiment again.

324
00:28:47,120 --> 00:28:53,680
I think I had this list of things I wanted to work on and sort of numbers I wanted to see with the trial conversion before I attempted it again.

325
00:28:54,480 --> 00:29:08,160
And one final thing that I'd like to do before we make that investment again is basically remove the need for email authentication because to date it has, but that required quite a big refactoring to remove that need.

326
00:29:08,720 --> 00:29:17,600
So basically seeing what that will do for the funnel as well because we don't have a high drop off on the email registration, but there is probably just like any other app.

327
00:29:18,080 --> 00:29:22,560
So I'd like to see that get those results for that in that funnel piece.

328
00:29:22,800 --> 00:29:26,000
And then I'm thinking early June start advertising.

329
00:29:26,400 --> 00:29:26,800
Awesome.

330
00:29:26,960 --> 00:29:30,720
And it's so cool to go into that from a position of strength with this organic.

331
00:29:31,200 --> 00:29:37,360
base versus I have to spend ten thousand a month or my apps just not getting any attention at all.

332
00:29:37,520 --> 00:29:44,960
So it's great that you kind of built the base first and then now you're going to kind of see if paid marketing can kind of accelerate that growth.

333
00:29:45,360 --> 00:29:46,800
Yeah, I did take a loss though.

334
00:29:47,360 --> 00:29:47,760
It's normal.

335
00:29:48,000 --> 00:29:53,840
I think I just tried it too early and it was like a big deal, like a big bummer at the time for sure.

336
00:29:54,400 --> 00:29:55,200
Yeah, totally.

337
00:29:55,680 --> 00:30:00,560
One of the things I noticed going through the app is that you do prompt people to share a lot.

338
00:30:01,040 --> 00:30:05,200
So right in the onboarding, it says, you know, better not do this alone, you know, share with a friend.

339
00:30:05,600 --> 00:30:08,880
And then on the daily quotes, you can share the daily quotes.

340
00:30:09,040 --> 00:30:15,280
And there's, you kind of like, find a lot of little ways in the app to incentivize that sharing or at least make it an option.

341
00:30:15,680 --> 00:30:18,960
Have you been tracking that and what kind of traction have you gotten from that?

342
00:30:19,760 --> 00:30:23,920
I am a big believer in also those, yeah, organic referrals.

343
00:30:24,320 --> 00:30:30,800
Some of our highest number of referrals actually come from doctors and therapists and folks that share it with folks in their communities.

344
00:30:31,040 --> 00:30:32,400
So that's been really, really cool.

345
00:30:33,040 --> 00:30:36,160
I'd say depending on the page, it performs differently.

346
00:30:36,640 --> 00:30:40,480
Probably the biggest share comes after a success page.

347
00:30:41,280 --> 00:30:44,800
There's a couple of screens and then you have the option to share rooted then.

348
00:30:45,040 --> 00:30:50,160
I think that's really that aha moment when a user has experienced, for example, relief from a panic attack.

349
00:30:50,640 --> 00:30:55,200
Then they are like, oh my God, I have to share this with so and so who I know also struggles with this.

350
00:30:55,440 --> 00:30:58,640
So yes, I think that's been a big piece of the growth as well.

351
00:30:59,040 --> 00:31:09,200
And then that's great because then when you start layering on that paid marketing, if you can make it work, then hopefully you've got a little bit of, you know, you're not just buying one user, you're buying 1.2 users or something.

352
00:31:09,360 --> 00:31:12,320
And it's a little bit of growth that's going to accelerate there.

353
00:31:13,120 --> 00:31:20,560
One last thing I wanted to touch on as we kind of wrap up is I think it's so cool that you have a little mascot, Ron, who's the little beast.

354
00:31:20,720 --> 00:31:23,680
Tell me about the branding and who Ron is.

355
00:31:24,320 --> 00:31:28,160
Yeah, so Ron is meant to represent anxiety and panic tax itself.

356
00:31:28,640 --> 00:31:34,560
So we talked about panic tax being something understandably very scary and terrifying.

357
00:31:35,440 --> 00:31:40,560
And what Ron does is represent it as something that is scary and uncomfortable at first.

358
00:31:41,120 --> 00:31:51,520
But as you go through the app, as you're working on improving yourself, you'll notice that the images of Ron are also getting a bit more smiling and a bit more welcoming and friendly.

359
00:31:52,000 --> 00:31:54,880
So you're taking something very scary and making it more friendly.

360
00:31:55,040 --> 00:32:01,200
And that's basically based off of cognitive behavioral therapy where you really want to get to know the thing that scares you.

361
00:32:01,360 --> 00:32:02,800
If there's no fear, there's no fuel.

362
00:32:03,200 --> 00:32:06,880
So he represents a lot and users actually personify Ron quite a bit.

363
00:32:07,280 --> 00:32:08,960
Some people say that he's their friend.

364
00:32:09,440 --> 00:32:10,800
He's there for them at night.

365
00:32:11,440 --> 00:32:18,080
You know, Rooted is meant to be a symbiotic tool to other tools like therapy and seeing your doctor, of course, very important.

366
00:32:18,960 --> 00:32:24,640
So Rooted and Ron are there when, you know, it's the middle of the night and you can't really call your therapist then.

367
00:32:24,960 --> 00:32:26,640
You have someone there and you don't feel alone.

368
00:32:26,800 --> 00:32:34,800
So he's been really beloved and it started off as an illustration in my journal again where I was having a day I was feeling so frustrated.

369
00:32:35,280 --> 00:32:39,040
So those more scary images of Ron were actually from back then.

370
00:32:39,600 --> 00:32:43,840
And then as I started getting better too, I was like, Oh, he just looks so cute when he's smiling like this.

371
00:32:44,000 --> 00:32:52,880
And I would start, I would go into these design holes and create a bunch of different emotions of Ron, and those are reflected especially in the journaling tool where you can select how you're feeling that day.

372
00:32:53,760 --> 00:33:02,720
It's so cool to have an app where that fits and you could have built this app without a mascot, without a little buddy in the app to help you along your way.

373
00:33:03,280 --> 00:33:04,800
But where do you see that going from here?

374
00:33:05,520 --> 00:33:15,680
I don't know that I could see the app without a little mascot because even from the very beginning it was like that needs to be an essential part of rooted.

375
00:33:16,320 --> 00:33:26,960
It was just really important to be guided along with someone because I think one of the worst things about panic attacks, especially back then when they weren't talked about much, is you feel so alone.

376
00:33:27,440 --> 00:33:30,000
You feel like you're the worst person ever to be experiencing this.

377
00:33:30,160 --> 00:33:31,120
This and nobody gets you.

378
00:33:31,280 --> 00:33:32,400
It's like very isolating.

379
00:33:32,720 --> 00:33:42,320
So to have any other, it's a self help app, of course, because you're not talking to a real person there, but to have any sort of representation of a character was like critical.

380
00:33:42,560 --> 00:33:43,920
Like it just needed to be in there.

381
00:33:44,320 --> 00:33:50,880
But where I see it going right now, I have many users who will write in asking for different ways to interact with Ron.

382
00:33:51,440 --> 00:33:58,800
So one really exciting feature coming up is there's actually going to be this daily mission, which is already in the app technically, right?

383
00:33:59,120 --> 00:34:00,880
You can complete your daily mission to get extra points.

384
00:34:01,280 --> 00:34:07,360
And those are really simple things for self care, like breathing, journaling, doing a really quick visualization.

385
00:34:08,080 --> 00:34:15,600
In the future, what it will be is Ron will be on the homepage and he will have a mood and you'll be able to take care of him kind of Tamagotchi style.

